Provenance
----------

⛔ CORRECTED 2026-09-09. This section used to end "A public specification, not
a copyrightable work; no third-party source was ported." THE SECOND HALF WAS
FALSE, and the same file contradicted it 80 lines further down, where it says
the two decode cores are "implemented as exact ports of the reference's
`reed_solomon_decode`/`reed_muller_decode`/`fft.c`". Four source files say the
same in their own headers: `reedsolomon.zig` ("mechanical port"),
`reedmuller.zig` ("direct, mechanical port"), `root.zig` and `prng.zig`
("transcribed from the reference"). The sources are right and the summary was
wrong.

⭐ WHY THE KIND IS STILL `provenance note`, AND WHY THAT DIVERGES FROM WHAT THE
AUDIT RECOMMENDED. The finding proposed re-tagging this file `third-party
attribution`. That would be wrong: the first line of a NOTICE in this repository
declares whether a CONDITION travels with the module, and none does. The
reference implementation is released into the PUBLIC DOMAIN — re-verified by
download 2026-09-09 from `gitlab.com/pqc-hqc/hqc` at tag `v5.0.0`, reproduced
verbatim below — so a consumer owes nothing, and declaring a condition that does
not exist would make the one line a reader is told to trust (`head -1
modules/*/NOTICE`, root `NOTICE` §1) less true rather than more.

It would also have a side effect nobody wants: `zig build check-copyleft` holds
only ATTRIBUTION files to its formal shape, so re-tagging a module purely to
look more cautious drags it under a gate for no reason. The defect here was
never the kind. It was two sentences that said the opposite of what the module
does.

hqc — the CODE is written from the official HQC specification ("Hamming
Quasi-Cyclic (HQC)", 22/08/2025, pqc-hqc.org — the spec accompanying NIST's
March-2025 selection of HQC as a code-based KEM backup to ML-KEM), with the
exceptions inventoried below: the two decode cores are ports, and several
tables and layout quirks are transcribed from the reference source.

Design reference (behavior/byte-layout only, no source copied): the
reference implementation, https://gitlab.com/pqc-hqc/hqc, tag `v5.0.0`
(public-domain license — see that repo's own LICENSE), consulted for
exact algorithmic behavior the spec's prose leaves underspecified:

- The `Xof.getBytes` 8-byte stream-consumption rounding quirk
  (`prng.zig`) — reference: `xof_get_bytes` in `src/common/symmetric.c`.
- The Barrett-reduction / 24-bit-rejection-threshold / duplicate-scan
  constant-access-pattern shapes of both fixed-weight samplers
  (`prng.zig`'s `sampleFixedWeightRejection`/`sampleFixedWeightBiased`)
  — reference: `src/ref/vector.c`'s
  `vect_generate_random_support{1,2}`/`vect_write_support_to_vector`.
- (Part 2) The GF(2^8) primitive polynomial and `exp`/`log` tables
  (`gf256.zig`) — reference: `src/ref/gf.c`/`gf.h`.
- (Part 2) The Reed-Solomon generator polynomial coefficients per
  parameter set and the systematic LFSR encode structure
  (`reedsolomon.zig`) — reference: `src/ref/hqc-{1,3,5}/parameters.h`'s
  `RS_POLY_COEFS` and `src/ref/reed_solomon.c`'s `reed_solomon_encode`.
  The decode algorithm implemented in `RS.decode` (constant-time
  Berlekamp-Massey, Gao-Mateer additive-FFT root-finding, Forney) is
  likewise behavior-referenced from `reed_solomon.c`'s
  `reed_solomon_decode` and `src/common/fft.c` (note: the additive-FFT
  root-finder lives under `src/common/`, not `src/ref/`, unlike the rest
  of the reference-implementation variant this module otherwise follows).
- (Part 2) The RM(1,7) generator-matrix encode structure and duplication
  (`reedmuller.zig`) — reference: `src/ref/reed_muller.c`'s `encode` and
  `reed_muller_encode`, `src/ref/data_structures.h`'s `rm_codeword_t`.
  The decode algorithm implemented in `RM.decodeSymbol` (expand-and-sum,
  fast Hadamard transform, find_peaks) is likewise behavior-referenced
  from `reed_muller.c`'s `expand_and_sum`/`hadamard`/`find_peaks`/
  `reed_muller_decode`.
- (Part 2) The RS-then-RM encode / RM-then-RS decode concatenation order
  (`code.zig`) — reference: `src/common/code.c`'s `code_encode`/
  `code_decode`.
- (Part 3) The HQC-PKE keygen/encrypt/decrypt byte layout and sampling
  order (`pke.zig`) — reference: `src/ref/hqc.c`'s `hqc_pke_keygen`/
  `hqc_pke_encrypt`/`hqc_pke_decrypt` and `src/ref/parsing.c`'s
  `hqc_ek_pke_from_string`/`hqc_dk_pke_from_string`.
- (Part 3) The Fujisaki-Okamoto implicit-rejection KEM transform's exact
  hash wiring and constant-time-selection mask arithmetic (`kem.zig`) —
  reference: `src/common/kem.c`'s `crypto_kem_keypair`/`crypto_kem_enc`/
  `crypto_kem_dec`, `src/ref/vector.c`'s `vect_compare`, and
  `src/ref/parsing.c`'s `hqc_c_kem_to_string`/`hqc_c_kem_from_string`.

Reproduced test data (⛔ NOT all of it "oracle output" — see below):
- Part 1: the same tag's `kats/ref/hqc-1/intermediates_values` file — a
  byte-exact, independently-verified fixture embedded in
  `src/kat_vectors.zig` (fetched via `curl` directly from gitlab.com and
  cross-checked in Python's `hashlib` before transcription, specifically
  to avoid hex-transcription risk from any summarizing intermediary).
- Part 2: no equivalent official fixture exists for the RS/RM/code layer
  in isolation in this tag (checked). Instead, the reference's own
  UNMODIFIED encode-side C source (`gf.c`, `reed_solomon.c`,
  `reed_muller.c`, `code.c`, `fft.c`, `crypto_memset.c`) was compiled
  locally with `gcc -O0` (one build per parameter set's own
  `parameters.h`, fetched via the GitLab raw-file and repository-tree
  API endpoints, not through any summarizing tool) and run against fixed
  test messages; the resulting stdout was mechanically converted to Zig
  array literals by a small Python script and embedded in
  `src/kat_vectors_code.zig`. No hex value in that file was hand-
  transcribed or LLM-summarized from the reference.
- Part 3: the official NIST-format KAT files,
  `kats/ref/hqc-{1,3,5}/PQCkemKAT_{2321,4602,7333}.rsp` (same tag),
  fetched via `curl` and parsed by a small Python script (no hand
  transcription, no LLM-summarized hex); the first 3 `count`s per
  parameter set are embedded in `src/kat_vectors_kem.zig`.

⛔ THE HEADING ABOVE USED TO SAY "Test-vector oracles (also design-reference,
not ported source)", and that mislabelled two of the three. Root `NOTICE` §0's
black-box-oracle carve-out covers "the program's observable behavior" and says
in as many words that it "stops at data that was never observed from a running
program at all — numbers read out of an upstream source file by a script are
reproduced data". By that line:

  * `src/kat_vectors_code.zig` IS oracle output — the reference C was compiled
    and RUN against messages we chose, and its stdout captured.
  * `src/kat_vectors.zig` is NOT — `intermediates_values` is a file in the
    upstream repository, read by a script.
  * `src/kat_vectors_kem.zig` is NOT — the `PQCkemKAT_*.rsp` files are likewise
    read, not observed.

Nothing is owed for either, because the upstream is public domain and the terms
are reproduced above. But calling read data "oracle output" is how a module with
a NON-public-domain upstream ends up quietly exempting itself, so the labels are
corrected here rather than left to be copied.

See SPEC.md's Verification section for exactly which primitives are
numeric-KAT-pinned against these fixtures vs. transcribed-from-source-only.

Nothing outside the inventory above was ported or consulted. Uses only the Zig
standard library (`std.crypto.hash.sha3`: SHAKE256, SHA3-256, SHA3-512).

Upstream terms, reproduced
--------------------------

Root `NOTICE` §0 asks that ported third-party source travel with its licence
terms reproduced, not merely cited. Until 2026-09-09 this file cited them
("public-domain license — see that repo's own LICENSE"), which sends the reader
to a file that is not in this tree. `gitlab.com/pqc-hqc/hqc`, tag `v5.0.0`,
`LICENSE`, fetched 2026-09-09:

    This software is released into the public domain.

    You are free to use, modify, and distribute this software for any purpose,
    with or without fee, and without any warranty.

    This project incorporates third-party code from the FIPS202 implementation
    used in the PQClean project, which is itself based on public domain code:

    - crypto_hash/keccakc512/simple/ from http://bench.cr.yp.to/supercop.html
      by Ronny Van Keer
    - TweetFips202 by Gilles Van Assche, Daniel J. Bernstein, and Peter Schwabe
      (https://twitter.com/tweetfips202)

    All of the included code is believed to be in the public domain.

⭐ Worth noting rather than eliding: upstream's own text says its FIPS202 code is
"BELIEVED to be in the public domain". This module ports none of that code — it
uses Zig std's SHA-3 — so the hedge does not reach here, and it is quoted rather
than trimmed because a licence quoted selectively is the defect `encoding`'s
NOTICE was carrying until the same day.

This is the complete arc (Parts 1-3): ring + PRNG foundation, the
complete RS/RM concatenated codec (both ENCODE and the two DECODE cores,
the latter implemented as exact ports of the reference's
`reed_solomon_decode`/`reed_muller_decode`/`fft.c`), and the PKE + FO-KEM
composition (`pke.zig`/`kem.zig`, byte-exact against the official NIST
KAT) — see SPEC.md's "Arc plan".
